{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"hypothesis","text":"The root process counts everyone who has ever lived. Its first-pass series ended at the Population Reference Bureau's 117.5 billion, but did not fit this model's other processes. (1) In 1950–2000 it added 14.5 billion births; population times birth rate gives about 6, since 90 to 135 million were born each year. (2) Before 1 CE the Bureau assumes about 80 births per 1,000 people a year, which in a population that barely grows means a life expectancy near 12 years; this model's h.alive says 25 to 30, which means 33 to 40 births per 1,000.

The series is now the integral of h.humanity times a crude birth rate consistent with h.alive: 34 per 1,000 for foragers, 37 to 40 for farmers, 35 to 38 in the 1800s, and falling from 37 in 1950 to 16.5 in 2025. Counted from about 320,000 years ago, it gives 22 billion by 1 CE and about 64 billion by 2025. The Bureau's account is kept inside the upper band at every date.

Predictions, recorded before checking: (a) published estimates built on palaeodemographic life tables with life expectancy above 20 should fall well below 100 billion; (b) the 1950–2025 increment is about 9 to 10 billion in any source using UN birth counts; (c) the two accounts differ almost wholly before 1650, so a check of births since 1650 cannot decide between them. What would decide it is the life expectancy of prehistoric and ancient populations. The tag is exploring until it is checked. EvidenceUnderdetermination#999b applies to the total, not to the modern increment, which the first pass simply had wrong.","data":{"sema":["sema:EvidenceDependence#mh:SHA-256:23ca19433d2e0bf6109a8334ef2a6d8a42124a01a802d5a8fe0c9a049a7b5582"],"rival":"Population Reference Bureau, How Many People Have Ever Lived on Earth? (as recalled, not consulted): about 117 billion by 2022."}}

{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"plan","text":"Continuing the construction imported from construction/history-numbers.construction.json (head graph a378b9af, model r1 509d4164), on 2026-10-09, with the live viewer open and Sema in use.

The pass fills the coverage report's schedule: level 3 (18 blocks, 6 new dates per process) and level 4 (54 blocks, 33 more), so every one of the 50 processes is guessed at 73 dates from 300,000 years ago to 2025. Each new value is guessed within its neighbours and, where a neighbour turns out to be wrong, the neighbour is revised openly. The convention of the first pass is kept: holder claude; tagged sketch before 1500 and inferred after, unless a value rests on something stronger or weaker; ice-core CO2 is inferred throughout. Bands are now judged per value: the first pass's formula had a floor that gave tiny quantities, such as population 100,000 years ago, bands ten times their value.

The patterns that check the guesses: MalthusianTrap#0f6b (output per person rises after a population loss, as after 1348), DensityRatchet#2ace (farming's longer hours and shorter people), SurplusBound#7fe3 (the share outside food-getting is bounded by the surplus), Sunbound#c94c (energy per person flat until fossil fuels), WaveOfAdvance#4cec (the spread of farming), and EvidenceDependence#23ca: nearly every figure here is recalled from one training corpus, so agreement between them is not independent support.","data":{"sema":["sema:MalthusianTrap#mh:SHA-256:0f6b99da46aa40c7bc427c5af4545b269837f66722ccfbc34994ab50e2e951da","sema:DensityRatchet#mh:SHA-256:2ace122db86434e8c46cc763c9cd5a6cc1842eec9205ac988f789cc5fefd99fc","sema:SurplusBound#mh:SHA-256:7fe3fb8cfb1870df417d716d1472f9153387d6c9e2938fc9204321a32675da94","sema:Sunbound#mh:SHA-256:c94c74970e9c2f8954dfd44e3844fc69bcda69ee43b8adda10c645c7e8e99764","sema:WaveOfAdvance#mh:SHA-256:4cec3c27911453d51cc5b750f2dd89c7894bf39e96f152ef810221b41f898ba0","sema:EvidenceDependence#mh:SHA-256:23ca19433d2e0bf6109a8334ef2a6d8a42124a01a802d5a8fe0c9a049a7b5582"]}}

{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"decision","text":"Requested as 'continue' on 2026-10-09; the first item of claude.plan.next-pass. Each Event is a shock or transition the curves already showed, named with the processes it changes, and each of those processes now carries values at the Event's start and end. That is 134 new values, the coarse curves turned into what each Event did.

War is a rate averaged to the next date, so every inserted date re-scopes the value before it. Neighbours were revised so the old averages are conserved where the old value was right: 1940's 300 over 1940–1950 becomes 500 over 1940–1945.7 and 50 after, which averages to 306. They were corrected where splitting showed the old value was wrong. 1600–1650 had held the Thirty Years War and the Ming–Qing wars at 120; with 1618 inserted, 1600–1618 is 20 and 1618–1650 is 180. 1859–1890 at 40 hid the Taiping war's deadliest years, now 200 over 1859–1864. A rate averaged to the next date is fragile under refinement; a next pass could store war as deaths per decade block instead, whose sum is conserved by construction.

Causal links are tagged by the claim's strength. Inferred: the deglaciation enabled farming, farming enabled states, the First World War spread the 1918 flu. Exploring: Termination II enabled the dispersal, and the Mongol conquests enabled the Black Death."}

{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"model","text":"Following METHOD.md (How Sema is used, part 3), each mechanism the Events instantiate is a Concept whose provenance names its card by full Sema id, a specialization of 'a recurring mechanism of historical change'. Card dependencies are relations: the structural-demographic cycle and the density ratchet build on the Malthusian trap, land-bound energy constrains it, and twin flow delivers virgin-soil epidemics.

Eleven episodes are claimed as Realizations. Inferred:
- the Black Death as MalthusianTrap#0f6b and Twinflow#9474;
- farming as DensityRatchet#2ace and, for Europe, WaveOfAdvance#4cec;
- the first states as SurplusBound#7fe3;
- the industrial revolution as the lifting of Sunbound#c94c;
- the Columbian exchange as VirginSoilEpidemic#d1fd and Twinflow#9474.

Exploring:
- the Antonine plague as a virgin-soil epidemic, if smallpox was new to Rome;
- the Han collapse and the Ming–Qing transition as StructuralDemographicCycle#8433. For the second, the Little Ice Age is an outside shock that the card excludes, so the claim depends on separating the two (ConfoundedAttribution#18ab).

Each Realization's provenance names the card failure mode it risks: 'germs absolve' for the Americas, 'surplus read as given' for the first states, 'energy as sufficient cause' for Britain.","data":{"sema":["sema:MalthusianTrap#mh:SHA-256:0f6b99da46aa40c7bc427c5af4545b269837f66722ccfbc34994ab50e2e951da","sema:DensityRatchet#mh:SHA-256:2ace122db86434e8c46cc763c9cd5a6cc1842eec9205ac988f789cc5fefd99fc","sema:SurplusBound#mh:SHA-256:7fe3fb8cfb1870df417d716d1472f9153387d6c9e2938fc9204321a32675da94","sema:Sunbound#mh:SHA-256:c94c74970e9c2f8954dfd44e3844fc69bcda69ee43b8adda10c645c7e8e99764","sema:Twinflow#mh:SHA-256:947477acf8435fa11be8a39a4d344d688445401036944becd6af0636d8f705f0","sema:WaveOfAdvance#mh:SHA-256:4cec3c27911453d51cc5b750f2dd89c7894bf39e96f152ef810221b41f898ba0","sema:VirginSoilEpidemic#mh:SHA-256:d1fd811afceaea483fe483c103b1c668e86d518625f8735bfa6328923597e878","sema:StructuralDemographicCycle#mh:SHA-256:84332fbf5a6c3b8266a43f1a1cfcd1f71e82cda8116a68b1de6656ed864bb158","sema:ConfoundedAttribution#mh:SHA-256:18ab53212e93905d346996f9ad168cee8cde1ac1d23bac5447298372e2a5f9a0"]}}

{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"decision","text":"Humanity is opened into the share of all living people in each UN M49 region (Africa, the Americas, Asia, Europe, Oceania), the same form the construction uses for the religions: shares of one unit that sum to 100 at every date. The structure construction (structure/history-tree.mjs) asks this same question at its leaves. Antarctica is left out: it has had no permanent population, and a series of zeros would add nothing.

The shares sum to 100 at each of 80 dates, the 73 of the schedule and the plague dates. Before 1500 they are sketches chosen between HYDE and McEvedy and Jones, which disagree for regions by a factor of two (Africa in 1500: 10 to 19%). From 1500 they follow Durand- and UN-type figures as recalled; none has been read, so none is tagged source.

What they show at once: Europe falls from 18 to 13.9% across the Black Death; the Americas fall from 11% in 1492 to 2.2% in 1650; Asia's share bottoms near 55% in the 1920s–30s, the European migration peak, and Europe's tops at 25% in 1910 before falling to 9% in 2025; Africa's share doubles from 8% in 1900 to 19% in 2025."}

{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"interpretation","text":"1. Conditions outweigh sparks. In almost every ranking the largest difference belongs to a condition that made the outcome possible, not to the trigger:
   - the Holocene for farming (0.85);
   - farming for states (0.85);
   - the Americas' isolation for their collapse (50 of 85 points);
   - coal for Britain's industry (0.45);
   - the industrial revolution for the escape from poverty (43.5 of 46.5 points).
   Triggers decide when, and matter less: the Natufian 0.2, the steam engine 0.3, smallpox 13 points. The exception is the Great Chinese Famine, where policy, not weather, made almost all of the difference (28 of 30 million).
2. The contrast changes the answer, as ContrastClass#7398 says. The Holocene makes a difference of 0.85 to farming's date and 0.01 to its place, because south-west Asia and Australia shared it. Wild cereals make 0.47 to the place and 0.05 to the date. The tool flags the Holocene and the deglaciation as making almost no difference to the place, which is the point rather than a case of two causes that would each have been enough.
3. Causes recur across outcomes. Mass schooling appears in six rankings (life expectancy, fertility, poverty, democracy, literacy); the second world war in two (democracy, the long peace); the Little Ice Age in three (the Black Death's arrival and toll, the Ming); Haber–Bosch in two. These shared causes are where a law or abstract relation could be stated next.
4. A transition can hinder another. The fertility transition stood in the way of population growth: without it, about 10.5 billion people added instead of 6.5. It is the only hindering cause besides aerosols on warming and vaccines on COVID-19's toll.
5. What drives a process changes over time. Children's infections are half of the life-expectancy gain before 1950 and 40% after; heart disease and stroke are nothing before 1950 and a fifth after."}

{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"model","text":"Two forms, by whether the change adds up.
- Non-additive questions (life_cause_record): 21 why-questions with 78 readings. Each names the outcome Event, its contrast and one measure: a probability, or an amount in the outcome's unit, often the change in a process (years gained, points lost). Each cause is read without itself, everything else as it was. The gap ranks the causes; it is never a share, since causes act together. Outcomes: the dispersal, farming (twice, for when and for where), the first states, the Black Death (twice, for its arrival and its toll), the collapse of the Americas, the industrial revolution, the Ming collapse, the 1918 flu, the Great Chinese Famine, the gain in life expectancy, population growth, the fertility transition, the escape from poverty, democratization, the long peace, the end of legal bondage, warming, literacy, and COVID-19's toll.
- Additive changes (Cuts on the outcome Event, answers naming the Events or processes that produced the parts):
  - the 41.5 years of life gained since 1900 by the fall in death that produced them, for the whole and for 1900–1950 and 1950–2025, weighted by years gained;
  - the 149 ppm rise in carbon dioxide by emission source;
  - the 15 points of forest lost by the clearing that removed them;
  - the 6.5 billion people added since 1900 by region. This one says where, not why.
The mechanism cards checked each reading: VirginSoilEpidemic#d1fd, Twinflow#9474, MalthusianTrap#0f6b, Sunbound#c94c, SurplusBound#7fe3, ExitCage#eef7, StructuralDemographicCycle#8433, DemographicTransition#6e31, DensityRatchet#2ace, CumulativeCulture#4f93. Where causes changed together, the reason names the rival account, as ConfoundedAttribution#18ab asks; this applies above all to the industrial revolution and the Ming. Every reading is tagged inferred or exploring and rests on recalled scholarship, not on computed counterfactuals.","data":{"sema":["sema:ContrastClass#mh:SHA-256:7398dcd341662f9a871586de9037c08c6a825113c47694f18d764e353ba55a77","sema:ConfoundedAttribution#mh:SHA-256:18ab53212e93905d346996f9ad168cee8cde1ac1d23bac5447298372e2a5f9a0","sema:VirginSoilEpidemic#mh:SHA-256:d1fd811afceaea483fe483c103b1c668e86d518625f8735bfa6328923597e878","sema:Twinflow#mh:SHA-256:947477acf8435fa11be8a39a4d344d688445401036944becd6af0636d8f705f0","sema:Sunbound#mh:SHA-256:c94c74970e9c2f8954dfd44e3844fc69bcda69ee43b8adda10c645c7e8e99764","sema:ExitCage#mh:SHA-256:eef79b28d91f7a5904c5526d70d0a0b3aa60e68b5fd2641420e425c848bd676b","sema:StructuralDemographicCycle#mh:SHA-256:84332fbf5a6c3b8266a43f1a1cfcd1f71e82cda8116a68b1de6656ed864bb158","sema:DemographicTransition#mh:SHA-256:6e31f50b19556b5b9d064d144b5d38ad11b5035daefc2c7d335a798f6f5e7023","sema:CumulativeCulture#mh:SHA-256:4f938b644cc8fd33afd520ea0e9c04e43b8156908b2db513a638e053f7e86dbe","sema:MalthusianTrap#0f6b","sema:SurplusBound#7fe3","sema:DensityRatchet#2ace"]}}

