{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"decision","text":"The people living in Sub-Saharan Africa is carved into 4 parts: The people living in Eastern Africa, The people living in Middle Africa, The people living in Southern Africa, The people living in Western Africa.

How the parts make the whole work: The four shares divide Sub-Saharan Africa's people at every date and sum to its share. Among foragers the continent's people were spread across all four, with Eastern and Southern Africa large; the Bantu-speaking farmers' spread from Middle Africa filled the east and south after 1000 BCE; the slave trades bled Western and Middle Africa most from 1650 to 1850 and conquest and the Congo Free State's regime struck Middle Africa again; since 1950 Eastern and Western Africa, with births still high, have grown fastest of all. Each part's turn comes at a different date, which Africa's one share hid.

Routing sample: 7 case(s); 0 with no home among the parts; 0 with two or more parts as homes.","data":{"schema":"meaning-model-carve/v1","part":"synthesis","cutId":"carve.concept.humanity.africa.sub-saharan","conceptId":"concept.humanity.africa.sub-saharan","form":"processes","cutRecorded":true,"parts":[{"key":"eastern","conceptId":"concept.humanity.africa.sub-saharan.eastern","processId":"h.region-africa.sub-saharan.eastern"},{"key":"middle","conceptId":"concept.humanity.africa.sub-saharan.middle","processId":"h.region-africa.sub-saharan.middle"},{"key":"southern","conceptId":"concept.humanity.africa.sub-saharan.southern","processId":"h.region-africa.sub-saharan.southern"},{"key":"western","conceptId":"concept.humanity.africa.sub-saharan.western","processId":"h.region-africa.sub-saharan.western"}],"process":"h.region-africa.sub-saharan","routing":[{"case":"Omo Kibish in Ethiopia: Homo sapiens about 230,000 years ago","homes":["eastern"]},{"case":"Blombos Cave's engraved ochre, about 75,000 years ago","homes":["southern"]},{"case":"The Kingdom of Kongo, about 1390 to 1914","homes":["middle"]},{"case":"The Mali empire under Mansa Musa, about 1312 to 1337","homes":["western"]},{"case":"The rinderpest panzootic and the Ethiopian great famine, 1888 to 1892","homes":["eastern"]},{"case":"The Congo Free State, 1885 to 1908","homes":["middle"]},{"case":"The Witwatersrand gold rush draws migrant labour from across the region, from 1886","homes":["southern"]}]}}

{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"decision","text":"Scale of rule is kept whole, as a kind of A dimension of rule: No carve of the largest polity's share pays now: the candidates were kinds (which polity is largest, from the Achaemenids to China and India; the empire's core against its subjects) or sibling readings of scale (the share held by the five largest polities, the effective number of polities, territory), none a part that sums to it. Reopen it when a series of the share under colonial rule from 1500 or of the whole size distribution of polities is opened, since scale would then be a parent with readings to divide, or when its Events or unexplained moves grow by half.

How the parts make the whole work: The largest polity's share is one order statistic of how humanity is divided among polities. It has no parts that sum to it: what changes within it is which polity is largest, a sequence of kinds the Events already tell, and, for empires, the split between core and subject peoples, recorded as the kind empire under scale.

Dropped: Core people and subject peoples, Which polity is largest, The rest of the size distribution, Territory held.","data":{"schema":"meaning-model-carve/v1","part":"synthesis","cutId":"carve.concept.humanity.means.politics.scale","conceptId":"concept.humanity.means.politics.scale","form":"processes","cutRecorded":false,"parts":[],"process":"h.largest-polity"}}

{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"decision","text":"The greatest settlement is kept whole, as a kind of A dimension of how a population settles: No carve of the largest city pays now: the candidates were kinds (which city), a matter of definition (core against suburbs, fixed here as the built-up area) or a sibling reading of the settlement pattern (the share of humanity in cities of a million or more), none a part that sums to it. Reopen when that share is opened under habitat, or when the Events that move the largest city or its unexplained moves grow by half.

How the parts make the whole work: The largest city's population is one order statistic of how humanity settles; its history is told by which city held the title and why it could be fed, which the Events carry. What it hides, the spread of great cities beyond the largest, is a sibling reading for habitat, not a part.

Dropped: Which city is largest, Core and suburbs, The other great cities.","data":{"schema":"meaning-model-carve/v1","part":"synthesis","cutId":"carve.concept.humanity.means.habitat.largest-city","conceptId":"concept.humanity.means.habitat.largest-city","form":"processes","cutRecorded":false,"parts":[],"process":"h.largest-city"}}

{"schema":"meaning-model-understanding-note/v1","kind":"plan","text":"Done in this pass (2026-10-15, Meaning Model engine 0.13, Sema 0.10.0 with the project vocabulary store/sema/world-history.db). The model went from r95 (3401cfc2) to r157 (bb3c3d17). The graph was rebound to every model revision; from r101 to r125 it was rebound late, by the next carve, a lapse recorded in claude.finding.tree-pass-1.

The tree of processes went from 56 processes to 113. Every new process is a part of a recorded carve, with a measure, values over the whole span at all four levels of the time schedule, a band and a tag on every value, and a note where it turns. No existing process was filed under a new parent, and the six M49 areas and the existing measures keep their definitions.
- 28 carves are recorded with life_carve_record (frame, climb, the three tests for every candidate, completeness against an outside list, routed cases and synthesis). The first 15: history; humanity by place and by ends and means; Asia, the Americas, Latin America, Europe, Africa, Sub-Saharan Africa and Oceania into their M49 subregions; the Earth as setting; what matters to people; how people get it; staying alive; the causes of death. The next 13: meaning, sustenance, habitat, politics, provisioning, violence, war, the plant cover, voice, food, standing, connection, carbon dioxide.
- Two processes are kept whole with the reason recorded: the largest polity and the largest city.
- 57 processes are opened. Findings in claude.finding.tree-pass-1 and claude.finding.tree-pass-2.
- Values changed in existing measures: Bangladesh 1971 and Rwanda 1994 added to war and safety, the gap claude.plan.next-pass-8 named.

Left open, in order of what it would explain:
1. Re-filing, the choice this pass was told not to make. Kinds and second readings filed directly under a parent are flagged as untested parts: the eight religious shares (kinds of tradition, h.meaning.tradition), foraging (a kind of food-getting) and the food-work share (food-getting read by labour), both under h.provisioning, being online (a kind of moving words) under h.connection, and the development index under h.wants. So is the larger choice between the structure's twelve dimensions (acut.way-of-living) and the ends-and-means tree the measures hang on.
2. Divisions still without a recorded carve, each a single child: freedom (voice passed; civil liberties or women's legal autonomy would need a world series to make a second part), understanding (schooling; numeracy from age heaping is a candidate), belonging (marriage; extended-family or one-person households are candidates) and play (mass media; reading matter or the arts are candidates).
3. Frontier parts recorded with their verdicts, to promote when a world series can be read:
- the Earth: freshwater, nutrient cycles, methane and aerosols;
- the body: mental health;
- the means: know-how, the wealth stock, unpaid household work;
- meaning: the experience of the sacred, religious specialists;
- sustenance: clothing, household goods;
- the habitat: built land;
- politics: law, form of rule;
- violence: execution, which needs the safety measure widened, and violence that does not kill;
- the forests: open covers, planted against primary;
- freedom and voice: civil liberties;
- standing: women's standing, esteem;
- connection: migration.
Sibling readings worth opening: the share living in cities of a million or more, the share under colonial rule from 1500, the effective number of polities.
4. Value questions found and not settled:
- war's pre-modern peaks seem to include famine and disease deaths, which hunger and infection count, and war leaves out China's campaigns of 1950 to 1953 and the Cultural Revolution's killings;
- the forager child mortality against adult survival;
- Oceania's share from 1500 to 1800;
- energy for warmth counts food energy.
5. The deepen check continues with sea level (by ice sheet), government revenue, toil and exchange, and with the religious kinds once re-filed.
6. Coverage. The schedule is filled at all four levels for all 113 processes, but 105 processes pass over the model's own Events, above all 1940 to 1945 and 1960 to 1970. That is the coverage pass's work.
7. The repository. README.md and METHOD.md still describe the construction before the Americas pass, and construction/history-numbers.construction.json and construction/MANIFEST.json hold it at r58. Exporting with life_construction_export and updating both texts would publish this pass and the two before it.
8. Sources. Every reading is recalled; the first to check are the shares behind the new parts (output sectors, food sources, forest domains, wealth) and the death tolls behind the parts of war.
9. The model's unweighed causes: the Roman Empire, the Antarctic Treaty, the rise of Christianity, the French Revolution."}
